  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric  equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+6\\pi n,\\,x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\,\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi , & \\,\\frac{x}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+6n\\pi , & \\,x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+6n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$",
